// PRACTICAL EXAMPLE: what "safe access" is, now that a name must exist
//
// This file used to be built on a pattern that was retired on 2026-08-19: create
// a variable inside a block and then ask `variable#?` whether it exists. Asking
// a name its type is not an exception to "defined before use", so that is an
// error now, and the LSP had been saying so all along while the CLI stayed quiet
// (reject/variables/01_inspect_undefined.zy).
//
// What replaces it is not a workaround, it is the same idea written where the
// reader can see it: give the name a value first, and let the branch change it.
// The default stops being implicit in "the variable was never created" and
// becomes a line you can read.

>> "=== Example 1: a value that a branch may replace ===" ¶

create_optional = #1        // change to #0 to take the other branch
optional_var = ""           // the default, stated

? create_optional {
    optional_var = "This variable exists!"
}

? optional_var$# > 0 {
    >> "Using optional variable: " optional_var ¶
}
_{
    >> "Optional variable not set, using default" ¶
}
¶

>> "=== Example 2: a default value ===" ¶

user_choice = 0             // 0 means "the user chose nothing"
? user_choice > 0 {
    >> "Using user choice: " user_choice ¶
}
_{
    >> "Using default value: 50" ¶
}
¶

>> "=== Example 3: #? still inspects — of a name that exists ===" ¶

// `#?` is for asking what a value IS, which is a different question from
// whether a name was ever created. On a name that exists it answers the type
// symbol, the element count and the value itself.
dato = [1, 2, 3]
info = dato#?
>> "  symbol: " info[1] ¶
>> "  count:  " info[2] ¶

texto = "hola"
>> "  string: " texto#? ¶
¶

>> "=== All practical examples completed ===" ¶
